course: biology
lesson: 45
title: More record-breakers and oddballs: sloth, narwhal, chameleon, electric eel, giraffe
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every record-breaking body is an answer to a problem: the sloth is slow because leaves give little energy, the giraffe is tall to reach leaves nobody else can, and the electric eel makes shocks to hunt in muddy water.
explain: A giraffe's neck looks as if it must have extra bones, but it has 7, exactly as many as yours; each one is just very long, up to about 25 cm, longer than a new pencil.
explain: Imagine being so slow that green algae grow in your hair and moths live in it, then remember that the same sloth can swim across a river about three times faster than it climbs.
explain: Cartoons get these animals wrong all the time: the chameleon turns tartan on a picnic rug, the sloth is lazy, and the narwhal's tusk is a unicorn horn; the real reasons are better stories.
fact: Sloths move only a few metres a minute and save energy with a slow body and a low body temperature, because the leaves they eat give little energy and take weeks to digest.
fact: Algae grow in grooves in sloth fur, giving it a greenish tint that may help it hide from harpy eagles, and sloth moths and beetles live in the fur.
fact: A sloth climbs down about once a week to poo, and it swims about three times faster than it moves on land, holding its breath for up to about 40 minutes; calling it lazy is a myth.
fact: The narwhal's tusk is a tooth, usually the left upper canine, that grows in a spiral through the upper lip up to about 3 metres long; mostly males have one, and it has millions of tiny nerve channels.
fact: In the Middle Ages narwhal tusks were sold in Europe as unicorn horns and were worth more than their weight in gold.
fact: Narwhals dive to about 1,500 metres or more under the Arctic sea ice to hunt halibut, squid and shrimps, and must find cracks in the ice to breathe.
fact: Chameleons change colour mostly to signal to other chameleons and to warm up or cool down, not to match any background; a cold chameleon turns darker to soak up more sunlight. It does this by changing the spacing of tiny crystals in their skin.
fact: Each chameleon eye moves on its own, so it can look forward and backward at once; to catch an insect both eyes turn forward and its tongue shoots out, sometimes longer than its body.
fact: An electric eel can make shocks of up to about 860 volts in one kind described in 2019, from many small electric cells firing together; it is really a knifefish, related to catfish and carp, not a true eel, and it lives in muddy South American rivers.
fact: Electric eels breathe air and must gulp at the surface every few minutes, and they send out weak electric pulses to sense things in murky water.
fact: A giraffe has 7 neck bones like almost all mammals, each up to about 25 cm long; sloths and manatees are among the few mammals with a different number.
fact: A giraffe is up to about 5.5 metres tall; its heart weighs about 11 kg, strong enough to push blood all the way up the long neck to its brain, and to drink it must splay its legs, which leaves it open to lions, so it often gets water from leaves instead.
fact: A giraffe's tongue is about 45 to 50 cm long and dark blue-black, which may protect it from sunburn, though scientists are not certain; it wraps the tongue round acacia leaves to pull them from between the thorns.
exercise: Why are sloths so slow? | Their leaves give little energy, so they save it by moving slowly | They are just lazy and do not want to move unless they really have to | Their legs are broken from hanging | They are always asleep because of the heat
exercise: Why is sloth fur often greenish? | Algae grow in grooves in the hairs | Sloths roll in green paint | Sloths eat so many leaves that they turn green | It is green from birth like a frog
exercise: How often does a sloth climb down from its tree? | About once a week, to poo | Every hour, to drink from a stream | Never once in its whole long life | Every night, to sleep on the ground
exercise: How does a sloth do in water? | It swims about three times faster than it moves on land | It sinks straight to the bottom like a stone and has to be rescued | It cannot hold its breath at all | It floats but cannot move
exercise: What is a narwhal's tusk? | A long spiral tooth growing through its upper lip | A horn on its forehead like a rhino's, made of hair | A bone sticking out of its nose | A fin that grew hard and pointed
exercise: What were narwhal tusks sold as in the Middle Ages? | Unicorn horns | Teeth of dragons | Elephant tusks | Combs of mermaids
exercise: How deep can narwhals dive? | About 1,500 metres or more | About 15 metres, just under the waves | Only just under the ice, never deeper | All the way to the centre of the Earth
exercise: How does a narwhal breathe under the Arctic ice? | It finds cracks in the ice and breathes at the surface | It breathes through gills under water, like a fish does | It stores air in its tusk | It never needs to breathe
exercise: Why does a chameleon turn bright colours when another chameleon comes near? | It is sending a signal, like «go away» or «I am strong» | It is trying to blend in by copying the other chameleon's colours | It is getting ill | It is too hot and about to faint
exercise: What can a chameleon's eyes do? | Move separately, so it looks forward and backward at once | See straight through leaves and branches to find hidden flies | Glow in the dark | Only look straight ahead
exercise: How does an electric eel make a big shock? | Many small electric cells fire together and add up | It eats batteries in the river | It rubs its skin hard against the rocks to build up a charge | It catches lightning from storms
exercise: What is an electric eel really? | A knifefish, related to catfish and carp | A true eel, just like the eels that live in the sea | A water snake | A kind of shark
exercise: Why does an electric eel come to the surface? | It breathes air and must gulp every few minutes | To catch flies on the water | To warm up in the sun | To recharge its electricity from the sunlight on the water
exercise: How many bones are in a giraffe's neck? | 7, the same number as in ours | 70, one for every 7 cm of neck | 24, more than any other animal | 1, one single long bone all the way down
exercise: Why is drinking dangerous for a giraffe? | It must splay its legs and lower its head, so lions can attack | Water makes its neck far too heavy to lift back up again quickly | It might swallow fish by mistake | It cannot swallow with its head down
exercise: How does a giraffe eat acacia leaves among thorns? | It wraps its long dark tongue around them | It knocks all the thorns off first with its two short horns | It pulls the tree down with its neck | It waits for the leaves to fall
test: «sloths are just lazy lol, imagine sleeping all day» What is right? | Slowness saves energy, because their leaves give so little of it | Yes, they are simply lazy | Yes, and they are ill most of the time | No, sloths are actually the fastest climbers in the whole rainforest
test: In a cartoon a chameleon on a picnic rug turns into a perfect tartan. What does a real one do? | Changes colour mostly to signal or to warm up or cool down | Copies any pattern it sits on, even tartan, stripes and spots | Turns see-through | Stays the same colour all its life
test: «narwhal tusk = unicorn horn, so unicorns were real» What is right? | The tusk is a narwhal's tooth, once sold as a unicorn horn | Yes, unicorns lived in the Arctic | Yes, narwhals are sea unicorns with horns of bone on their foreheads | No, the tusk is made of ice
test: «giraffe's got a long neck so it must have like 50 neck bones» What is right? | It has 7, like us; each bone is just very long | Yes, about 50, that's why it bends so well | It has about 25, like a snake | It has no bones in its neck at all
test: Do you think a mouse has more or fewer neck bones than a giraffe? | The same, 7 | Fewer, about 2 | More, about 20 | None at all, it is too small
test: What would happen if a giraffe's heart were as weak as ours for its size? | Not enough blood would reach its brain high up | It would grow even taller | Nothing, any heart at all can pump blood that high up | It would have to drink twice as much
test: «don't touch the eel tank mate, it's like a socket» Why is he right? | An electric eel can give a shock of hundreds of volts | Eels bite with poisonous fangs | The tank is full of sea water that burns your skin off | Eels can jump out of the tank and strangle you
test: In a game, the electric eel lives in clear deep sea water and breathes with gills only. What is wrong? | It lives in muddy rivers and must breathe air at the surface | Nothing, that is correct | It lives in the desert | It lives in the sea but breathes through a hole in its tail like a whale
test: A chameleon is cold in the early morning. What colour change helps it? | Turning darker to soak up more sunlight | Turning white to stay cool | Turning bright green to hide from birds | Turning see-through
test: Why could a green coat be useful for a sloth in the treetops? | It may help it hide among leaves from harpy eagles | It keeps it warm | It makes the leaves it eats taste much better | It glows at night
test: «why does that chameleon look two ways at once, is it broken» What is right? | Its eyes move on their own, so it can watch all around | It is about to faint | It has a nasty eye disease and needs a vet | Every animal can do that, you just never noticed
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the slowness of the sloth? | It conserves the little energy afforded by a diet of leaves | It is a consequence of idleness without biological purpose | It results from the weight of algae accumulated in the fur | It is caused by an inability to sleep
exam: Identify the principal nature of the tusk of the narwhal. | It is a tooth, usually the left upper canine, protruding in a spiral through the lip | It is a horn arising from the centre of the forehead | It is a modified fin of hardened skin | It is a bone of the nose extended forward
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the neck of the giraffe. | It contains seven bones, as in almost all mammals, each being very long | It contains a greater number of bones than that of any other animal | It contains a single elongated bone | It contains no bones, being supported by muscle alone
exam: Ascertain the principal purpose for which chameleons alter their colouring. | Signalling to other chameleons and regulating body temperature | The exact imitation of any background upon which they rest | The attraction of insects to their tongue | The repair of damaged skin
